# Product and Project workspace boundaries

Status: candidate hosting/classification revision, 7 November 2026. Authority effect: none.

Use **Product Workspace** and **Project Workspace** as local display labels, registered under workspaces/products/ and workspaces/projects/. The [current catalog](WORKSPACES.md) distinguishes active Lab candidates, external workspaces, retained history and embedded Projects.

## Scope and hosting

| Kind | Responsibility |
| --- | --- |
| Product Workspace | Durable Product purpose, value, capabilities and evolution, with references to its contributing Projects. |
| Project Workspace | Independently accountable initiative, Goals and Work, referencing a Product or another declared purpose. |

Both use Product, Project, Work, Authority, Ontology, Architecture, Implementation and Documentation responsibilities. A referenced home can satisfy a responsibility without copying its records. A Product and Project can share a physical home while preserving distinct identities and authority.

## Current hosting choices

- The Lab Product stays at repository root.
- Craft, Define, Ontology, People and Teams, Lab website, Trusted Dataset Materialization and the referenced Agent Reasoning Engine scope remain in the active Product map with their existing candidate/source standing.
- No separate Project Workspace is currently selected. Craft development is Project coordination inside Craft. Definition Formal Assurance is Project coordination inside the Lab root; the theorem-checker plan is related Lab Work.
- TROQAR, Engineer Assessment and Agentic Research Network have external homes. Their Product/Project subjects are not separate hosted Lab workspaces.
- Ontology Lifecycle is an existing Project and implementation package inside one proposed Ontology Product Workspace. See [the consolidation](ONTOLOGY-WORKSPACE.md).
- Definition NFT and Lab migration are removed from active workspace planning. Historical source/admission records remain retained as described in [the cleanup result](CLEANUP-20261107.md).

Each Product uses one physical operating home for its Product, Project and Work responsibilities. The theorem checker has a plan/work-pack but no canonical Project identity selected here. No physical move is performed by this mapping. Define/website have Product documentation; People and Teams has a shared domain model. Those sources do not automatically establish native Product/workspace admission.

## One Product home

Apply the [one Product home rule](PRODUCT-COLOCATION.md) to all scopes, including the Lab itself and external Products. Separate identities do not justify duplicate workspaces. The [complete folder structure](FOLDER-STRUCTURE.md) places coordination and Work inside each Product.

## Shared operations

Both kinds use shared Lab operations by default. Behavior specialization and dedicated runtime placement are independent choices. Workspace creation does not require another runtime installation, copied capability code or separate database.

Runtime dispatch must resolve selected workspace/subject, exact revision, applicable Project context and real operation permissions. The [earlier candidate](../../docs/proposals/0012-lab-workspace-model/SHARED-RUNTIME.md) is still Project-only; Product-wide dispatch and kind-specific cardinalities are specified as a [successor revision](MODEL-REVISION.md).

## Goals, people and teams

A [Goal](../../authority/definitions/candidates/0006-craft-identity/definitions/goal/v1/definition.json) remains a desired outcome owned by one Project. Craft ORODI integration keeps its original Goal and membership posture; it is not renamed into a Project Workspace. Plans, tasks, models and capability folders do not automatically become workspaces.

People and Teams is one candidate capability-development boundary because the source models share a declared semantic scope. Individual Persons, Teams, memberships and Roles remain records. A separately accountable initiative can use a Project Workspace that references its participants.

## Boundary criteria and migration

Use purpose, accountable owner, scope/exclusions, attributable state, lifecycle, compatibility and recovery needs to decide whether a scope needs independent hosting. A separate process/directory by itself is insufficient. Canonical homes retain their own semantic/lifecycle ownership even inside one workspace.

Before relocation, select exact subject identities, source records, primary homes, shared dependencies, applicable authority, model revisions, extension scope, consumer compatibility and historical retention. The [migration map](MIGRATION-MAP.md) gives the earlier region-level inventory; today's hosting choices are in the current catalog.

The catalog changes no canonical Definitions, native identities or frozen Define Packs. The two-kind operating-model successor remains explicit work.
